#import "../../../../../shared/environments.typ": *

ME/CFS patients encounter pramipexole almost exclusively through the RLS comorbidity. The paper's existing position — developed in @sec:full-partial-agonist-d2-d3 and the medication compendium (@sec:medication-reference, Pramipexole entry) — is that pramipexole's D2/D3-selective full agonism can worsen ME/CFS symptoms even while treating RLS, because it stimulates the D2-mediated "brake" pathway without activating the D1 "go" pathway — it strengthens brake tone in a system whose D1 "go" tone the model places on the left (suboptimal) arm of the inverted-U — a pre-existing speculation, not a measurement (no cited ME/CFS cohort measured D1 tone), so the D1/D2 balance tilts further toward the brake. The side-effect prediction at RLS doses carries on the D2/D3 overstimulation account alone; the D1-reduction clause is the framework's compositional reading, not a measured premise. The evidence question this subsection addresses: what does the cross-disease literature actually show when a full D2/D3 agonist is given to people with fatigue, and what does it say about ME/CFS specifically?

#clinical-finding(title: [The Analog Evidence for Pramipexole and Fatigue Points in Different Directions])[
There is no pramipexole (or any dopamine-agonist) trial in ME/CFS or long COVID — this is a structural null, verified across PubMed, Europe PMC, and Crossref in this cycle's search. The cross-disease analogues split into two directions.

*Direction: improvement.* Ventorp et al. (2026, Nature Medicine) randomised 85 adults with anhedonic depression to pramipexole add-on or placebo (double-blind, 9 weeks, plus 6-month open-label extension): the SHAPS anhedonia scale improved by −4.04 vs placebo (95% CI −6.89 to −1.18, $p$ = 0.006, Hedges $g$ = 0.62), with PET-verified reward-related ventral-striatal target engagement, light physical activity increased, and improvement sustained through the extension; the pramipexole dose is not stated in the cited source (the source annotation records flexible dosing) @Ventorp2026PramipexoleAnhedonia. Browning et al. (2025, the PAX-D trial) randomised 151 patients with treatment-resistant unipolar depression to pramipexole add-on or placebo (multicentre, double-blind, 48 weeks, titrated to a 2.5 mg target; mean 2.3 mg at week 12): the QIDS-SR16 week-12 between-group difference was −3.91 (95% CI −5.37 to −2.45, $p$ \< 0.0001) @Browning2025PAXDPramipexoleTRD — the largest controlled trial in this protocol line; its dose is far above the 0.125–0.5 mg RLS range and its primary endpoint is the depression axis (QIDS-SR16), with no fatigue measure; 48-week treatment termination for adverse events was 20% (15/75) on pramipexole vs 5% (4/75) on placebo (nausea, headache, sleep disturbance/somnolence). Morita et al. (2011) found pramipexole exposure independently associated with lower fatigue prevalence in 350 Parkinson's patients (cross-sectional) @Morita2011PramipexoleFatigue. Holman et al. (2005) ran the only DA-agonist RCT in a fatigue syndrome — fibromyalgia: pramipexole 4.5 mg nightly (a Parkinson's-range dose, roughly 10× the RLS range) improved pain (VAS −36% vs −9% placebo), ≥50% pain reduction (42% vs 14%), and fatigue (29% vs 7% on the MDHAQ fatigue item) in a comorbidity-rich cohort in which comorbidities and disability were explicitly not excluded, stable concomitant analgesics were allowed, and no patient withdrew for inefficacy or an adverse event at the full-agonist dose @Holman2005PramipexoleFibromyalgia. Fibromyalgia's dopaminergic receptor state is not characterized in this paper — the dose caveat, not a state assignment, is the basis of the discount applied to this trial. Bruno et al. (1996) reported 3/5 post-polio fatigue patients improving on bromocriptine (placebo-controlled crossover, n = 5) @Bruno1996BromocriptinePostPolio. Dose-band asymmetry: the benefit-side data sit above the 0.125–0.5 mg band where the dose is reported (PAX-D mean 2.3 mg at week 12; Holman 4.5 mg) or at an unreported dose (Ventorp — the dose is not stated in the cited source), while the harm-side claim is pinned to the 0.125–0.5 mg band — the predicted within-patient dissociation is therefore a cross-dose extrapolation, and no dataset in any dose band shows both halves coexisting in one patient.

*Direction: null or worse.* Oved et al. (2006) measured objective fatigability in 15 PD patients before and after 3 months of dopamine agonists: the objective fatigue index was unchanged, while Epworth somnolence rose from 6.6 to 11.7 ($p$ \< 0.05) @Oved2006DAAgonistFatigueSomnolence. Samuels et al. (2006) tested pramipexole 0.5 mg — the top of the paper's RLS working band (0.375 mg, exactly at the Takahashi augmentation threshold, is 75% of this ceiling) — in 16 healthy males (double-blind crossover): pramipexole reduced alertness (pupillographic sleepiness and critical-flicker-frequency) and raised heart rate — an alertness/somnolence readout, not a fatigue measure — whereas modafinil 200 mg did not @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil.

*Synthesis.* The direction of "pramipexole on fatigue" is not resolvable from analogies because the populations differ in the variable the receptor-state framework predicts determines agonist direction: their dopamine receptor state — Parkinson's disease is terminal loss (low tonic drive at damaged terminals); the Ventorp 2026 and PAX-D depression cohorts had no supersensitivity rationale; the ME/CFS framework posits D2/D3 supersensitivity from chronic deficiency. This mapping is a *post-hoc interpretation, not a pre-registered discriminator*: it does not predict the PD split (Morita's improvement and Oved's null are the *same disease*, which the variable cannot separate), it assigns the depression cohorts to a slot defined by the *absence of a rationale* rather than a measured state, and the same directional split is partially explained by simpler variables (endpoint: prevalence vs fatigability; dose: 4.5 mg vs 0.5 mg; disease). The analogues disagree rather than concur — the conflict is a verified literature finding, and the state mapping is the framework's best reading of it, not a prediction the framework delivered. The asymmetry also cuts the other way: the positive direction's strongest studies measured *reward and depression* (SHAPS anhedonia scale; QIDS-SR16), not fatigue — the transferable claim is "motivated reward behaviour is drug-modifiable", and the untransferable claim is "ME/CFS fatigue improves"; the one analogue that measured objective fatigability directly (uncontrolled, pre/post, n = 15) found no change and a somnolence rise @Oved2006DAAgonistFatigueSomnolence. The two design classes cut opposite ways and cannot be traded against each other: the uncontrolled pre/post study is the only direct fatigability measure but has no control arm for the change, while the controlled RCTs measured reward, depression, and pain axes at doses far from the RLS band — endpoint directness and design/dose-range caveats point in opposite directions, so neither direction is priced as decisive.

(Translation gap: all four populations (mood disorder, PD, fibromyalgia, post-polio) → ME/CFS. Not yet validated in ME/CFS patients. Each analogue's certainty is lower than this synthesis: 0.45 (Ventorp), 0.25 (Morita), 0.35 (Holman), 0.20 (Bruno); the conflict statement itself is a verified literature finding.)
(Certainty: 0.55 for the *existence and stability of the conflict* as a verified literature finding. Per-analogue certainties: 0.45 (Ventorp), 0.45 (PAX-D), 0.25 (Morita), 0.35 (Holman), 0.20 (Bruno), 0.25 (Oved — n = 15, pre/post, no control arm for the change), 0.50 (Samuels — double-blind crossover, healthy volunteers, no fatigue endpoint). Evidence type: RCT + PET (Ventorp); multicentre RCT (PAX-D); cross-sectional (Morita); RCT (Holman); crossover pilot (Bruno); pre/post (Oved); double-blind crossover PK/PD (Samuels). Population: cross-disease, none ME/CFS. Replication: the two directions are mutually non-replicable by construction (different populations, endpoints, designs, and dose bands — the receptor-state mapping is the unmeasured post-hoc reading above) — the conflict's stability is definitional, not an empirical claim, and the 0.55 attaches to the existence of the conflict, not to the stability label. The receptor-state mapping above is the meta-framework's own interpretation at its own certainty (0.25, @sec:ligand-receptor-duality), and its premise is contested by normal CSF DA indices in post-infectious ME/CFS (@Aregawi2026Noradrenergic) — disclosed here rather than left to the spec. Severity applicability: unknown — no analogue cohort stratified by ME/CFS severity; the Holman cohort's tolerability at the 4.5 mg full-agonist dose is a cross-disease high-dose tolerability signal (fibromyalgia; comorbidities and disability not excluded) — not evidence about severe/bedbound ME/CFS, where PEM and unmonitored OH are the relevant variables.)

*Consequence:* No honest summary of the analog evidence can recommend pramipexole for ME/CFS fatigue itself — the evidence is directionally inconsistent across populations. What the evidence does support is the RLS indication, with quantified risks, and it makes the receptor-state framework the *working* discriminating variable — the same drug's net effect in an ME/CFS patient depends on that patient's D2/D3 receptor state — at the framework's own certainty (0.25–0.35, @sec:ligand-receptor-duality), not at this environment's 0.55 literature-map certainty; 0.55 attaches to the existence of the conflict, not to the framework. The nomination is a working choice, not a consequence: the one within-disease test available (Morita's improvement vs Oved's null, both PD) cannot be separated by the variable, so the discriminating status rests on the framework's own inference. The side-effect profile at low dose is the probe of that state (@sec:ligand-receptor-duality).
] <cf:pramipexole-fatigue-analogue-conflict>

#speculation(title: [Full D2/D3 Agonism May Worsen Core ME/CFS Symptoms While Treating RLS in the D2-Supersensitive Phenotype])[
*Certainty: 0.35.* Scope: this claim applies to the DA-deficient / D2-supersensitive subset of ME/CFS; for the DA-preserved phenotype (selective central noradrenergic deficiency with preserved dopaminergic function — see hypothesis registry; @Aregawi2026Noradrenergic) the supersensitivity substrate is absent and the claim does not apply. In ME/CFS patients whose D2/D3 receptors are supersensitive (the chronic-deficiency upregulation model of @sec:dopamine-receptor-classification), pramipexole's full agonism (D3 > D2 > D4 affinity; negligible D1/D5) drives those receptors to maximal activation without the D1 "go" co-activation that endogenous dopamine provides. The predicted net effect at RLS doses (0.125–0.5 mg/day): RLS symptoms improve (the indication responds), while core ME/CFS symptoms — fatigue, effort allocation, motivation — do not improve and may worsen, with an exaggerated side-effect signature (nausea, dizziness, somnolence, sleep attacks) at doses that are subclinical for the supersensitivity prediction — the reference exposure (0.5 mg in receptor-normal men) produced measurable alertness and cardiovascular effects, so the subclinicality is scoped to the predicted supersensitive signature, not to all effects @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil.

Supporting strands: (a) the receptor-state framework itself (meta-framework certainty 0.25, @sec:ligand-receptor-duality); (b) the author's own case — D2 supersensitivity *indicated* (certainty ≤0.35, the parent framework's own cap, @sec:ligand-receptor-duality) by LDA akathisia at microdose, and pramipexole (Sifrol) produced significant adverse effects (nausea, dizziness, worsened fatigue) rather than relief when used for the secondary RLS-phenotype (@sec:full-partial-agonist-d2-d3, case illustration). This is the n = 1 anchor the framework leans on most; its competing explanations (peak pharmacokinetics; illness attribution — (c) and (d) below) bind at every site where the case is deployed, and the anchor's weight is capped by the ≤0.35 supersensitivity ceiling; (c) Oved 2006 — DA agonists raise somnolence without reducing objective fatigability in PD @Oved2006DAAgonistFatigueSomnolence; (d) Samuels 2006 — pramipexole 0.5 mg is sedating in receptor-normal healthy volunteers, so a supersensitive system should show at least this (the least-specific strand: it supports the supersensitivity reading only by the "at least this much" inference, and the same datum is also competing explanation (f)) @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil; (e) feed-in from the terminal-loss line: if dopaminergic terminal loss lowers evoked quantal release, chronic postsynaptic D2 supersensitivity is the expected downstream state — the full-agonist liability predicted here is the receptor-level expression of that line (@cf:vmat2-longcovid, @spec:structural-metabolic-vmat2).

*Competing explanations (equally presented):* (a) Ventorp 2026 shows the same full agonist improving anhedonia and motivation with PET-verified target engagement in a non-supersensitive population — the "full agonist worsens" claim is state-dependent, not drug-intrinsic, and could fail to apply if an ME/CFS patient's system is not actually supersensitive @Ventorp2026PramipexoleAnhedonia. (b) Morita 2011 and Holman 2005 report the improvement direction in PD and fibromyalgia respectively @Morita2011PramipexoleFatigue @Holman2005PramipexoleFibromyalgia. (c) The author-case adverse reaction may be an ordinary peak pharmacokinetic effect (area postrema + cardiovascular D2 at the Cmax) rather than a disease-specific supersensitivity effect — the ligand-receptor duality framework distinguishes these as Pattern-1 exaggeration versus standard peak effect (@sec:ligand-receptor-duality). (d) Illness-attributed attribution: in a fatigued, treatment-exhausted patient, any new drug's side effects are experienced against a high symptom baseline. (e) The premise itself is contested: in post-infectious ME/CFS, CSF dopamine-pathway indices (DA, DOPAC, HVA) are *normal* with a selective noradrenergic deficiency @Aregawi2026Noradrenergic — without a chronic tonic DA deficit, the deficiency-driven upregulation that the supersensitivity model requires has no documented cause in this population (the same counter-evidence is recorded against the trial-only augmentation framework, @spec:dopaminergic-augmentation-mecfs). (f) The generic drug-effect account: pramipexole 0.5 mg — the top of the RLS working band — reduces alertness in receptor-normal healthy males @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil, so worsened fatigue at RLS doses needs no ME/CFS-specific mechanism (the n = 1 author case is equally consistent with this).

*Falsifiable prediction.* In a within-patient crossover in ME/CFS patients with confirmed RLS, pramipexole 0.25–0.5 mg/day (RLS dosing) vs an alpha-2-delta ligand (guideline-concordant comparator): RLS improves under both arms (the indication is effective). The confirming pattern is the within-patient dissociation — RLS improves under both arms *and* core symptoms worsen under pramipexole relative to the alpha-2-delta arm on the non-reward measures (FSS, PEM diary frequency/severity). The anhedonia scale is the model's own predicted-benefit axis (the Ventorp SHAPS target above): a worsening there is confirming, but an improvement there is the forecast, so the anhedonia limb is reported separately from the falsification measures and does not by itself falsify or confirm the worsening claim; "unchanged" core symptoms on the non-reward measures are consistent with the model but also with generic non-efficacy on core symptoms, so they confirm only weakly, and a pure null (neither arm improves RLS) violates the premise and confirms nothing. Falsified if pramipexole improves core symptoms on the non-reward measures (FSS, PEM diary) at least as much as the alpha-2-delta arm (the "worsening" model is wrong for ME/CFS and the author case is an individual outlier). The full-vs-partial side-effect dissociation is the falsifier of the ligand-receptor framework generally, not of this spec — it is evaluable only in a design with a partial-agonist arm, which @spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation carries, not in this two-arm crossover. The low-dose aripiprazole arm (0.25–1 mg) is a separate claim with its own falsification condition (@spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation), and both full-vs-partial comparisons in that spec carry the Tadori 2011 density-dependence caveat (the partial-agonist cap is weakest in the supersensitive state it is meant to protect). Entry stratification on "the supersensitive ME/CFS patient" uses the side-effect probes (NTD, microdose akathisia) that this crossover is designed to validate — the design is circular on that axis as specified; the non-circular leg of the trial is the arm comparison itself, which does not presuppose the phenotype, so a probe-derived stratification is an exploratory subgroup, not the primary question. Attribution: the first failure condition (core-symptom improvement on the non-reward measures at least as much as the alpha-2-delta arm) is attributable to this spec; the full-vs-partial dissociation condition is attributable to the LDA-combo claim and is not evaluable in this design.

(Severity applicability: unknown — no ME/CFS data at any severity level; the framework applies most strongly to the D2-supersensitive phenotype, which has not been measured in any ME/CFS severity group.)
(Origin: mechanistic framework + single author case + cross-disease analogues; no ME/CFS trial data.)

*Consequence:* For a patient deciding between an alpha-2-delta ligand and pramipexole for RLS in ME/CFS, the honest summary is: the guideline says alpha-2-delta first; the receptor-state framework says the patient who reacts strongly to low-dose dopaminergic drugs (akathisia on LDA microdose, or nausea/dizziness at 0.125 mg pramipexole) has just identified a phenotype in which the full agonist is the higher-risk choice — a ranking that rests on the in-vitro single-ligand density model (Tadori 2011) at the supersensitivity claim's ≤0.35 cap, not on a measured in-patient comparison — and that side-effect reaction is itself the diagnostic data, not just a nuisance. For the partial-intolerant patient (akathisia on the LDA microdose — the author case), the NTD yardstick does not exist: there is no tolerated partial-agonist dose to measure against, and the readout degenerates to the initial-dose response profile, which is the documented harm datum (@sec:ligand-receptor-duality).
] <spec:pramipexole-full-agonist-worsening-mecfs>

#practical-warning(title: [Pramipexole in ME/CFS: Dosing, Monitoring, and Discontinuation Specifics (RLS Indication)])[
*Status.* Pramipexole is approved for RLS; its use in ME/CFS is for the RLS comorbidity, not for ME/CFS core symptoms (no ME/CFS trial exists; core-symptom benefit is not expected — see @cf:pramipexole-fatigue-analogue-conflict). The experimental dopaminergic-augmentation framework for ME/CFS apathy is separate and trial-only (@sec:dopaminergic-augmentation). Per the 2025 guideline, the first step in RLS management is addressing exacerbating factors — alcohol, caffeine, antihistaminergic, serotonergic, and antidopaminergic medications, and untreated obstructive sleep apnea — before and during dopaminergic treatment @Winkelman2025RLSguideline.

*Starting dose and titration.* IR is the implementable RLS route: 0.125 mg once at bedtime, titrating by doubling every 4–7 days. The ER formulation is not a starting option for RLS: its lowest strength (0.375 mg once daily) is ≈0.375 mg/day by steady-state equivalence — three times the IR RLS starting dose and exactly at the quantified augmentation-risk threshold @Takahashi2017RLSaugmentation, the doubling titration rhythm cannot run inside the 0.5 mg/day ceiling, and, as marketed, the RLS schedule is implemented with IR (a product-marketing observation — no label source is cited here) — ER use for RLS is an off-label decision near the top of the usable band, not a starting option (@cf:pramipexole-ir-er-pk). RLS ceiling: 0.5 mg/day — the augmentation risk threshold quantified at 0.375 mg/day (Takahashi 2017) means every step above it is a risk decision, not a convenience step @Takahashi2017RLSaugmentation @Winkelman2025RLSguideline. Titration runs over ~2–3 weeks with 3–4 supervised clinician contacts; no self-directed titration at any severity. For severe or very severe (bedbound) ME/CFS patients: no self-directed titration — the OH/somnolence/ICD risks are the same and monitoring is harder (a bedbound patient cannot report symptomatic OH or get out of a hypotensive episode); the substitute for the standing test is the maximum-feasible orthostatic assessment (supine→seated→standing, or supine→seated if standing is not possible) with pre-syncopal symptoms recorded as the orthostatic-hypotension surrogate (operational inference, not a validated rule), and palliative RLS treatment should run only under active clinical supervision with the assessment documented before and at each step (same logic as @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety).

*ME/CFS-specific monitoring.* (1) Orthostatic intolerance: DA agonists lower blood pressure; POTS affects 25–50% of ME/CFS patients @Newton2008POTSprevalence and broader orthostatic intolerance is estimated at 70–90% in this paper (@sec:orthostatic-mechanisms) — document supine/1-min/3-min standing BP+HR at baseline and at each titration step, with a hold-and-rehydrate plan for acute OH (shared with the L-DOPA framework in @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety). (2) Somnolence: pramipexole reduces alertness at 0.5 mg in healthy volunteers @Samuels2006PramipexoleModafinil and raises Epworth somnolence in PD @Oved2006DAAgonistFatigueSomnolence; ME/CFS patients with hypersomnia overlap (abnormal MSLT, high PLMS) are the highest-risk subgroup — no driving or machine operation until tolerability is established, and the sudden-sleep-onset warning from the DA-agonist label applies. (3) Impulse control: baseline QUIP-RS and monthly for the first 3 months; note that switching to or from aripiprazole does not reduce ICD risk (@cf:pramipexole-lda-combo-evidence) — screen regardless of agent. (4) Efficacy: IRLS at baseline, at each titration step, and monthly — the only efficacy readout in the indication; decision line: a documented per-step drop in IRLS versus the patient's own baseline, with the "meaningful" threshold agreed at baseline (no paper-level IRLS MCID figure exists — the operational rule replaces the number); if the agreed drop is not met within the days-to-2-weeks response window (allowing ≤4 weeks at a tolerated dose), the working exit is a structured taper and switch to an alpha-2-delta ligand, not continued escalation. (5) Core symptoms: FSS (or the paper's fatigue scale of record) and PEM-diary frequency/severity at baseline and at each titration step — core-symptom worsening under pramipexole is the model-confirming signal (@spec:pramipexole-full-agonist-worsening-mecfs) and a reassess/taper trigger in its own right, independent of the IRLS readout. (6) Augmentation: the RLS augmentation signature (earlier-in-the-evening onset, proximal spread to the thighs/torso, lost night coverage) checked at each titration step and monthly — the signature the 0.375 mg/day threshold quantifies (9.1% of pramipexole-treated RLS patients; @sec:medication-reference, Pramipexole entry) — present → stop escalation and taper per the discontinuation protocol below, not continue the titration. (7) Iron: assess ferritin and transferrin saturation (TSAT) before and during treatment — the AASM 2025 iron rule (consensus, 'not empirically tested' per the guideline itself): replete with oral or IV iron if ferritin ≤75 ng/mL or TSAT \<20%, IV iron only if ferritin 75–100 ng/mL @Winkelman2025RLSguideline; the paper's own ME/CFS iron rule governs the repletion decision and overrides the guideline trigger where they collide: functional iron deficiency (ferritin >150 µg/L with TSAT \<20%) receives no oral repletion (ferroptosis risk) and IV iron is an untested-in-ME/CFS 0.30 inference outside the AASM IV window (ferritin 75–100 ng/mL) — in that collision zone the default is no repletion, recheck, and treat the driver; genuine deficiency is ferritin \<30 ng/mL or TSAT \<16% with normal CRP (@sec:iron-redox-cascade; repletion target ferritin >100 ng/mL, @subsec:iron-repletion-neurodivergent). Units: ng/mL and µg/L are numerically identical for ferritin, so the guideline's ng/mL triggers and the paper's µg/L rule are directly comparable. (8) Co-prescriptions: the interaction table in @sec:pramipexole-coprescriptions — the modafinil and caffeine items most commonly asked about. (9) Renal: baseline CrCl/eGFR, then tracked — pramipexole clearance tracks creatinine clearance @Wright1997Pramipexolepk; no label/SmPC source is cited here, so no label renal rule is asserted — the operational inference from the cited PK is: at reduced CrCl, extend the titration interval rather than the dose step, and recheck at each step (operational inference, not a label rule). Sex-specific PK: in the cited study women clear pramipexole 24–27% more slowly and show 35–43% higher AUC (the sex effect is confounded with age and renal function in that source) — consequence: start at the floor of the starting dose and use the long end of the titration interval (operational inference) @Wright1997Pramipexolepk. (10) Pregnancy/lactation: no safety data; does not cross the safety threshold for use (same position as @warn:dopaminergic-augmentation-safety). (11) Mood: baseline mood screen and at each titration step for the first 3 months — the cited basis is the RLS head-to-head's ideation counts (6/3/2, @Allen2014PregabalinPramipexole) and the PAX-D termination rate (20% vs 5%); worsening mood or new ideation = reassess/taper. Owner allocation: the prescriber owns the lab and step items at the supervised contacts; the patient keeps the daily logs (PEM diary, the augmentation questions, mood notes) between contacts.

*Time to response.* RLS: days to 2 weeks. Core ME/CFS symptoms: no benefit expected — if a patient attributes improved energy to pramipexole, the first question is whether the RLS-related sleep restoration (not the dopaminergic effect) is the actual mechanism, since pramipexole's own fatigue-direction effects in analogues are conflicted in direction across populations — improvement (cross-sectional and RCT settings), null on objective fatigability, sedation on the alertness readout (@cf:pramipexole-fatigue-analogue-conflict).

*Discontinuation.* Never abrupt. DA-agonist withdrawal syndrome (DAWS: anxiety, paresthesia, insomnia, autonomic distress) is documented in the RLS-dose literature — the paper's cited anchor is a single RLS case report (n = 1) @Dorfman2013DAWSRLS — and mimics an ME/CFS crash — a documented "did the disease worsen?" confounder after any DA stop. The structured RLS-dose protocol (rapid DA taper + iron optimisation + alpha-2-delta start/increase, with partial-agonist adjunct use associated with success) achieved complete DA withdrawal in 77% of an RLS cohort with clinically significant DA augmentation (n = 31) over one month @MartinGarcia2026DAWSRLS. For an ME/CFS patient, the working protocol adds four modifications (pharmacological inferences layered on the RLS evidence, not ME/CFS-validated): (1) a slower taper than the RLS protocol — halving the dose at 1–2 week steps rather than over days, because the crash-mimicry risk is higher when the baseline illness is ME/CFS; (2) orthostatic vitals at each step, continuing the monitoring already required during treatment; (3) a PEM diary running through the taper, with an explicit discrimination rule — DAWS presents within days of a dose cut with anxiety/panic/autonomic predominance and *no* exertion trigger, whereas a PEM crash is exertion-linked with the characteristic 3–9 day lag (@sec:full-partial-agonist-d2-d3); deterioration fitting the DAWS pattern is managed by slowing or pausing the taper (or adding the partial-agonist adjunct — the 0.25–2 mg low-dose aripiprazole window @spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation, initiated by the prescriber with explicit off-label consent; the adjunct is a bridge, not a destination — it is maintained while the pramipexole dose halves at 1–2 week steps, and it is the pramipexole halving, not the adjunct, that runs the taper; akathisia is monitored as the adjunct's exit criterion — it occurred in 16.7% (mild/transient) of the cited rescue cohort @GarciaMalo2026AripiprazoleRLSAugmentation and is the same Pattern-1 signal the adjunct is meant to cap, so an adjunct-emergent akathisia exits the adjunct, not the taper plan; for severe/bedbound patients the adjunct carries the same supervision bar as the titration itself), not by attributing it to disease progression; (4) iron and alpha-2-delta coverage continued through the taper as symptom bridges @MartinGarcia2026DAWSRLS @Winkelman2025RLSguideline.

(Certainty: per item — the guideline-grade RLS items (dosing, titration, 0.5 mg/day ceiling, iron, co-prescriptions) are 0.60–0.70; monitoring items 1–4 (orthostatic, somnolence, ICD, IRLS) are cited-data monitoring at 0.50–0.60; the ME/CFS-specific monitoring items (core symptoms, augmentation, mood) are framework-level inferences layered on the cited data, each ≤0.35; the four ME/CFS-specific taper modifications in the discontinuation protocol are pharmacological inferences layered on the RLS evidence, each ≤0.35 — the warning as a whole is not a single 0.60. Severity applicability: stated per item above; bedbound patients carry the highest unmonitored-OH risk.)

*Consequence:* A patient on pramipexole for RLS in ME/CFS gets a safe trial if the trial is bounded: lowest RLS starting dose, 0.5 mg/day ceiling, orthostatic and somnolence checks at each step, QUIP-RS screening, and a structured taper plan agreed before starting — with the explicit expectation that core ME/CFS symptoms are not what this drug treats.
] <pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety>
